          "snippet": "The Kripke-Putnam arguments challenge descriptivism by demonstrating that natural kind terms function as rigid designators, thereby supporting semantic externalism and essentialist conclusions. This position is defended through modal and epistemological reasoning regarding reference fixing, though it faces objections concerning empty terms and the alleged question-begging nature of deriving metaphysical facts from semantics."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "raptor-subset-off",
          "title": "rigid-designators",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rigid-designators/",
          "score": 0.5473326,
          "snippet": "Kripke argues that identity statements involving rigid designators, such as proper names for the same object, are necessarily true if they are true at all. This contrasts with non-rigid descriptions like 'the brightest non-lunar object in the evening sky,' which designate contingent truths because the described property could have belonged to a different entity. The concept of rigidity extends beyond simple naming to include kind terms and standard measurements, revealing how necessary identities can be established through contingent means."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "raptor-subset-off",
          "title": "rigid-designators",
          "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rigid-designators/",
          "score": 0.53158325,
          "snippet": "The passages argue that rigid designators establish necessary truths about identity and essence across possible worlds, distinguishing this concept from descriptivism and counterpart theory. This framework applies to proper names, natural kinds, and theoretical identities, allowing for a posteriori knowledge of contingent discoveries like the identity of Hesperus and Phosphorus. While Kripke's view defends direct reference without qualitative criteria, it faces ongoing debate regarding its coherence with essentialism and alternative semantic accounts."
        },
